    <h2>Two Beliefs That Cost You Money</h2>

    <p>Two opposite beliefs drive bad appliance decisions. The first is that a repair is always the frugal, responsible choice — so people pour $400 into a twelve-year-old refrigerator that dies again within the year. The second is the throwaway reflex: the machine made a noise, so replace the whole thing, and out goes a washer that needed a $150 pump. Both are wrong because both skip the one calculation that matters. Repair-versus-replace is not a philosophy; it is arithmetic, and the number changes with the appliance, its age, and the specific part that failed.</p>

    <h2>It's Just Arithmetic</h2>

    <p>The rule technicians actually use is the 50-percent rule crossed with age. If the repair costs more than about half of a comparable new unit, and the appliance is past roughly two-thirds of its expected lifespan, replace it — otherwise, fix it. Lifespans are known: refrigerators run about 10 to 13 years, washers 10 to 12, dryers 12 to 14. So a $180 repair on a 5-year-old washer is obvious, and a $550 sealed-system repair on an 11-year-old fridge is throwing good money after bad. The variable people miss is the part: a control board, a gasket, a heating element, or a pump is cheap and worth doing well into a machine's life, while a compressor or a cracked tub is usually the end.</p>

    <h2>How the Honest Shops Operate</h2>

    <p>A straight appliance shop runs the diagnosis and then tells you the truth even when the truth loses them the repair. They will say a dryer is almost always worth fixing because the common failures — element, rollers, belt, thermal fuse — are inexpensive on a machine that lasts well over a decade. And they will tell you to walk away from a compressor job on an aging off-brand fridge whose proprietary parts cost nearly what the unit did. The shops that aren't straight do this: they take every job, replace a $300 part in a machine that had a month left, and you are shopping for a new one anyway two months later, now out both amounts. The honest admission that defines a good tech is the willingness to say "don't fix this."</p>

    <h2>What to Watch For on the Phone</h2>

    <p>Watch for a shop that quotes a repair over the phone without diagnosing — real numbers come after someone sees the failure. Watch for one that never mentions the age or the replacement option, because a tech who only ever says "fix it" is selling parts, not advice. And watch for vague labor with no diagnostic-credit policy. In a commuter town like Hartselle — where a lot of households are driving to Decatur or Huntsville all day and a dead refrigerator is a genuine emergency, not a someday errand — the shop worth keeping is the one that comes fast and tells you the truth, because you will call them for the next three appliances too.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Appliance Repair Costs in Hartselle</h2>

    <p>Real ranges:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Diagnostic service call</strong> — about $75 to $125, usually credited toward the repair.</li>
      <li><strong>Common repairs</strong> — roughly $150 to $350 for a dryer element, washer pump, or fridge fan or gasket.</li>
      <li><strong>Major repairs</strong> — refrigerator compressor or sealed-system work runs higher, and is exactly where replacement often wins.</li>
      <li><strong>The honest verdict</strong> — free with a good tech: whether yours is a fix or a replace, before you commit.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The value is not just the wrench work; it is the judgment. A tech who saves you from a bad repair is worth more than the fifty dollars they undercharged.</p>

    <h2>When to Call, When to Shop</h2>

    <p>A refrigerator or freezer failure is a same-day call — there is food and money spoiling by the hour — and the local shops that run same-day service earn their repeat business right there. For a washer or dryer, you have a little more room to get a real diagnosis and weigh the repair-versus-replace math without rushing. Either way, get the actual diagnosis before you decide; the phone guess is where people talk themselves into the wrong choice.</p>

    <h2>Repair or Replace: What Hartselle Homeowners Ask</h2>

    <p><strong>Repair the fridge or replace it?</strong></p>
    <p>If the repair tops half the cost of a new one and the fridge is past two-thirds of its 10-to-13-year life, replace. A cheap part on a younger machine, fix it.</p>

    <p><strong>What's usually worth repairing?</strong></p>
    <p>Dryers almost always, most washer faults, and fridges with a gasket, fan, or board issue. Compressors and cracked tubs usually aren't.</p>

    <p><strong>What does it cost?</strong></p>
    <p>Service call $75 to $125, common repairs $150 to $350; major repairs run higher and often tip toward replacement.</p>

